[
  "[SCENE: Camera. Show the original phone recording.]\nI took my phone for a walk under the power lines.\nI was recording a few thoughts about software craftsmanship.\nWhen I got home, I heard a loud hum in the audio.\nThe video was about twenty-one minutes long.\nThe audio was about eighteen minutes long.\nI thought the phone had lost sync.\n[PAUSE]\nThis is The Sound Above: Minus Zero. I'm Mike Hall.\nI'll show what I checked, what I got wrong, and what worked on a second walk.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show the suggested command and the original recording.]\nI asked an AI tool how to repair the audio.\nIt compared the two track durations and suggested speeding up the audio.\nThe proposed setting was atempo equals 0.837.\nI tried it. The speech sounded slow and out of sync.\n[SHOW: Play a short before and after sample.]\nThe command ran. The result was wrong.\nI needed to find out what those extra three minutes contained.\n",
  "[SCENE: Inspect the end of the video and the audio waveform.]\nI checked the point where the audio ended.\nI said \"Thank you\" and finished speaking.\nThen I kept walking with the camera running.\nThe last few minutes were silence, not a sync problem.\n[SHOW: Play the end of the original recording.]\nThe duration difference came from the walk after I finished talking.\nStretching the audio had changed speech that was already in sync.\n[PAUSE]\nThe recording answered the question. I had to listen to it.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show the two audio channels and their spectra.]\nThe phone recorded two audio channels.\nOne channel carried much more of the hum.\nThe other had clearer speech.\nI isolated the clearer channel and reduced the remaining hum.\n[SHOW: Compare the channels before and after cleanup.]\nI wanted to know whether those steps would work on another recording.\nSo I went back to the same trail with the same phone.\n",
  "[SCENE: Run the same cleanup on the second recording.]\nThe second recording had the same kind of hum.\nI used the same channel selection and cleanup steps.\nThe speech stayed clear, and the hum came down.\nI did not stretch the audio.\n[SHOW: Compare the original and cleaned audio.]\nThe second recording showed which parts of the first repair were useful.\nThe duration ratio was not.\n",
  "[SCENE: Outro card with the recording and source links.]\nI thought the recording was out of sync.\nListening showed that I had walked in silence after the talk.\nThe second recording helped me test the audio cleanup separately.\nA useful check is simple: compare the output with the source.\nThe recording, notes, and code are linked from just3ws.com.\nNext, we go back to the UGtastic interviews with Sergio Pereira.\nThanks for watching.\n"
]